# Every function the `munsu` binary cannot reach, and why it is allowed to stay.
#
# `.github/scripts/deadcode.sh check` compares this file against
# `deadcode ./cmd/munsu` in both directions: unreachable in the tree but missing
# here is red, and listed here but reachable (or gone) is red too. So a new
# function that nothing in the binary can call cannot land quietly, and a line
# here cannot outlive the function it waives.
#
# This is a ratchet. It is only supposed to get shorter. Adding a line is
# allowed and sometimes right; doing it without a reason anyone would accept is
# how the file turns into a graveyard, and the reason column is self-declared,
# so reading the diff is what catches that -- not the script.
#
# Columns are tab-separated: file <TAB> function <TAB> reason
#
# No line numbers on purpose: they are not part of an entry's identity, and
# including them would rewrite this file on every unrelated edit above a
# function. Run `.github/scripts/deadcode.sh list` for the current keys.
#
# The set is the union across every GOOS in .github/build-tags.manifest, so a
# GOOS-gated file is covered even though only one build ever compiles it. That
# is why the linux and darwin halves of a `_linux.go`/`_darwin.go` pair both
# appear below, and why this file reads the same on a laptop and on the runner.

# --- Known open bugs, NOT accepted debt -------------------------------------
# A guard that exists, is complete, and cannot fire because nothing in the
# binary calls it. These are holes, not debt: the code was written to refuse
# something and the refusal can never happen. They are listed here only so the
# lane can go green; `deadcode.sh check` reprints every one as a warning on
# every single run, and each line names the issue that owns the verdict --
# wire it up or delete it. A line leaves this section with that issue, not
# before, and nobody may quietly demote one to accepted debt.
#
#   BEO-102  the writer/endpoint fence computes exactly the quiescence proof a
#            destructive teardown needs -- terminate exactly, rescan, then set
#            VerifiedQuiescent -- and nothing calls it. `captain retire
#            --remove-home` reaches os.RemoveAll having checked provenance,
#            meta, and an artifact scan that --force skips outright, and never
#            verifies at the OS-process level that the directory it is about to
#            delete has stopped being written to.
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	ServiceEndpointController.DisposeBoundEndpoint	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	ServiceEndpointController.ProbeBoundEndpoint	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	TaskEndpointScanner.ScanEndpoints	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	fenceEndpoints	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	metaUint	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	readEndpointMeta	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	sameBoundEndpoint	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/endpoint_fence.go	validBoundEndpoint	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/writer_fence.go	CompositeWriterFence.FenceOSWriters	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/writer_fence.go	CompositeWriterFence.FenceWriters	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/writer_fence.go	correlateWriters	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it
internal/fleet/writer_runtime.go	FenceBoundEndpoints	OPEN-BUG(BEO-102): writer/endpoint fence computes the quiescence proof a destructive teardown needs, and nothing calls it; captain retire --remove-home runs os.RemoveAll without it

# --- Not findings: GOOS-union artifacts --------------------------------------
# The set is the union across every GOOS, so a function reachable on one
# platform and unreachable on another lands in the union. These two are live
# code on the platforms that call them; there is nothing to burn down and no
# issue to open. Intersecting the platforms instead would be the wrong fix --
# it would hide a genuinely dead function in any GOOS-gated file.
#
# A platform-gated file whose function is unreachable on the very platform that
# compiles it is NOT this case. Those are ordinary debt and stay below.
internal/fleet/process_args.go	splitProcessCommand	GOOS-union artifact: live on darwin via process_inventory_darwin.go; the linux and windows builds have no caller for it
internal/fleet/process_args.go	writerKindForArgs	GOOS-union artifact: live on linux and darwin via process_inventory_{linux,darwin}.go; only the windows build cannot reach it

# --- Accepted debt, imported as the baseline --------------------------------
